{
  /* ==============================================================================================
   * The EMIT / PACKAGING program for the extracted Slatwall Catalog slice: the same rules as
   * ./tsconfig.json over a narrower program — shippable src/** only, with emit switched on.
   *
   * The division of labour with the base configuration:
   *   tsconfig.json        type-checks src/**, test/** and jest.config.ts, and never emits.
   *   tsconfig.build.json  emits src/** only, so nothing test-only can reach a Lambda artifact for
   *                        the simplest possible reason: nothing test-only is in the program.
   *
   * Program scope and emit are the only differences between the two files.
   *
   * `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json` consumes this file. The bundler does not: build/esbuild.mjs derives
   * its inputs from the src/handlers entry points plus the import graph and applies its own platform
   * and format flags. So this program proves the shippable set type-checks and emits cleanly under
   * the same strict contract as the gate that approved it, and the bundler then packages it.
   *
   * Every path below is subtree-relative and none walks up out of this directory, so no program
   * defined here can reach the CFML tree.
   * ============================================================================================ */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* --- INHERITED, AND NEVER WEAKENED -------------------------------------------------------
     * Everything about the language and the type system comes from ./tsconfig.json and is
     * deliberately absent below. Inherit and narrow; never duplicate. Duplicating the compiler
     * options would let the two files drift, and a drifted build config would ship an artifact
     * checked less strictly than the gate that approved it — precisely the false assurance this
     * configuration exists to prevent. No relaxing option may ever be added here. If a file under
     * src/ fails to compile, THE SOURCE IS FIXED. `tsc --showConfig -p tsconfig.build.json` prints
     * the resolved set, which is how to prove the inheritance is intact rather than assume it.
     *
     * No module path aliases are declared here and none exist in the base: every intra-subtree
     * import is relative, because esbuild does not honour tsconfig alias maps and an alias that
     * type-checks could fail to resolve at bundle time, throwing MODULE_NOT_FOUND on cold start
     * where no compiler is left to catch it.
     *
     * The five options below are the entire content of this file — two overrides that turn a
     * type-check program into an emit program, and three emit settings restated at their inherited
     * values because this is the only program that emits. */

    /* OVERRIDE 1 of 2 — emit. The base sets noEmit true so that `npm run typecheck`, and any bare
     * `tsc -p tsconfig.json`, can never scatter JavaScript into dist/. This is the one
     * configuration that flips it, which is exactly what makes the base's setting meaningful. */
    "noEmit": false,

    /* OVERRIDE 2 of 2 — emit layout. The base uses rootDir "." because its program spans src/,
     * test/ and jest.config.ts. Here the program is src/ alone, so rootDir "src" makes dist/ mirror
     * src/ directly rather than nesting under dist/src/. build/esbuild.mjs sets outbase to src/ for
     * the same reason, so a path in a stack trace means the same thing whichever step produced the
     * JavaScript. */
    "rootDir": "src",

    /* Restated at the inherited value; never divergent. outDir is an emit concern and this is the
     * emitting program, so the destination is pinned where emission is switched on. Three artifacts
     * must agree on this name: the `dist/` entry in .gitignore, this option, and the outDir in
     * build/esbuild.mjs. */
    "outDir": "dist",

    /* Source maps are what let a stack trace from a deployed invocation point back at the
     * TypeScript that produced it. build/esbuild.mjs sets sourcemap for the same reason, so both
     * routes to an artifact stay debuggable. */
    "sourceMap": true,

    /* No .d.ts output. The package is private and the deliverable is bundled Lambda artifacts, so
     * nothing outside this subtree consumes it as a typed library and declaration files would be
     * unread build products. declarationMap is absent for the same reason. */
    "declaration": false
  },

  /* Program scope — the defining purpose of this file: emit excludes tests.
   *
   * `include` admits shippable TypeScript under src/ and nothing else. `exclude` states the same
   * boundary from the other side so the intent survives any later edit to `include`.
   *
   * build/esbuild.mjs is absent from both lists because it is .mjs and belongs to no TypeScript
   * program, and no pattern walks up out of this directory.
   *
   * One caveat worth knowing when auditing what ships: `exclude` filters the initial file glob and
   * cannot remove a file that an included file imports. Keeping test-only code out of the emitted
   * output therefore also depends on nothing under src/ importing from test/ — which the hexagonal
   * layering already implies, since test doubles are injected through constructors rather than
   * imported by production classes. */
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "coverage",
    "test",
    "**/*.test.ts",
    "**/*.spec.ts",
    "jest.config.ts"
  ]
}
